    {
     "tipo": "explicacion",
     "titulo": "Follow the argument, not just the topic",
     "texto": [
      "**Three things to look for.** In an opinion article, look for the main idea, the **argumentos** that support it and the **objeciones** the author admits.\n\n**Connectors.** **Sin embargo** introduces a contrast; **por ejemplo** gives an illustration; **por tanto** marks a **conclusión**; **además** adds a reason.\n\n**Claiming is not proving.** An opinion needs a source, an example or a comparison. Words like **algunos**, **en general** or **puede** limit what is said: they stop a **tendencia** from sounding like a rule.\n\n**This text** is a practice article: its **propuestas** are the writer's opinion, not any company's rule."
     ],
     "tutora": ""
    },
    {
     "tipo": "gramatica",
     "titulo": "Conceding and qualifying: por más que and como si",
     "texto": [
      "**Por más que** admits a difficulty and carries on. With the **indicative** it talks about something that really happens: **Por más que lo busco, no encuentro ningún dato.** With the **subjunctive**, about something possible: **Por más que parezca urgente, habrá que evaluarlo.**\n\n**Como si + imperfect subjunctive** compares with something unreal: **Sostiene el café como si fuera un pequeño sol.**\n\n**Connectors** to organise the argument: **sin embargo** (contrast), **por ejemplo** (illustration), **por tanto** (conclusion), **además** (addition)."
     ],
